data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":260,"item_id":"msg_cbb90073c4684ecdbb8fbb835ac385b5","output_index":0,"content_index":0,"text":"# Practical Guide to Tending a Small Vegetable Garden Through Spring to Winter\n\n## Introduction\n\nStarting a small vegetable garden in the spring through winter is a rewarding and sustainable way to grow food in your own backyard. Each season offers unique challenges and opportunities, and with careful planning and consistent care, you can thrive in every part of the year.\n\nThis guide provides a detailed practical approach for tending a small garden through spring, summer, autumn, and winter. It includes specific advice for each season, helping you build a strong and healthy garden throughout the year.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Spring: Starting the Garden\n\n**What to Do:**\n\n1. **Plant Seeds and Start Seeds**\n   - Choose a variety of small vegetables that you can grow in a pot or in your own garden. Consider your space and the type of soil you have.\n   - Start with a small pot (1–2 feet in diameter) to avoid overwatering and soil compaction.\n   - Plant the seeds in well-drained soil. If you have a pot, fill it with compost or rainwater to enrich the soil.\n\n2. **Provide Nutrient-rich Water**\n   - Water your garden regularly, especially in the early spring. Ensure that your plants receive adequate moisture, but avoid over","logprobs":[]}

