data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":260,"item_id":"msg_9fd5212a58434bf58505d04c11cf0d30","output_index":0,"content_index":0,"text":"# Practical Guide to Tending a Small Vegetable Garden Through Spring, Summer, Autumn, and Winter\n\n## Introduction\n\nGrowing a small vegetable garden in the spring, summer, autumn, and winter is a rewarding and sustainable way to grow food in your own backyard. Each season offers unique challenges and opportunities, and with careful planning and consistent care, you can thrive in every part of the growing cycle.\n\nThis guide provides a detailed practical guide to tending a small vegetable garden through these seasons, including specific advice for each season.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Spring: Starting the Garden\n\n**What to Do:**\n\n1. **Start Seeds Early**  \n   Begin planting in early spring (around March to April) to give your garden a head start. Choose a variety of vegetables that you can grow in your own garden. For example, carrots, zucchini, or tomatoes.\n\n2. **Planting Techniques**  \n   - **Spacing**: Space plants at least 10–15 cm apart to allow proper growth.  \n   - **Planting Technique**: Plant the seeds in a well-drained pot or in the soil.  \n   - **Soil Preparation**: Ensure the soil is rich in organic matter (compost or well-rotd compost).  \n   - **","logprobs":[]}

