data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":260,"item_id":"msg_6f7641f1ec154f48807c2ee3ca55ec7b","output_index":0,"content_index":0,"text":"# Practical Guide to Tending a Small Vegetable Garden Through Spring to Winter\n\n## Introduction\n\nStarting a small vegetable garden in the spring through winter is a rewarding and sustainable way to grow food in your own backyard. Each season offers unique challenges and opportunities, and with careful planning and patience, you can thrive in every part of the year.\n\nThis guide provides a detailed practical guide to tending a small vegetable garden through spring, summer, autumn, and winter. It includes specific advice for each season, helping you keep the garden healthy and thriving.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Spring: Starting the Garden\n\n**What to Do:**\n\n1. **Start Seeds Early**  \n   Begin planting in early spring (around March to April) to give your garden a head start. Choose a variety of vegetables that you can grow in your own soil. For example, start with tomatoes, basil, and carrots.\n\n2. **Planting Techniques**  \n   - **Spacing**: Space plants at least 10–15 cm apart to allow proper growth.  \n   - **Planting Technique**: Plant the seeds in a well-prepared hole, making sure to plant the entire seed (not just the sprout).  \n   - **Soil Preparation**: Before planting, test your soil pH","logprobs":[]}

